# Reviewed gitleaks findings that are confirmed false positives.
#
# One fingerprint per line, in the form
#   <commit>:<path>:<rule-id>:<line>
# Pinning the exact commit+path+rule+line means an entry suppresses precisely
# one known finding and cannot mask a real credential elsewhere in the file --
# unlike a path or regex allowlist in .gitleaks.toml, which stays broad. Use
# this file for one-off historical findings; use .gitleaks.toml for a pattern
# that recurs by design (see the fake test-token allowlist there).
#
# Note this is about HISTORY, not the working tree. CI scans the PR's commit
# range, and a release PR (develop -> main) spans every commit since main last
# moved -- so a finding in a long-deleted file resurfaces on every release PR.
# Deleting the offending file does not clear it; only an entry here does.

# products/ngts/openapi.yml carried a vendor OpenAPI `example` block whose
# "privateKey" value is a placeholder: -----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY----- wrapping a
# run of U+22C6 STAR OPERATOR characters, not base64 and not a key. The file was
# deleted in c505977, but the blob stays in history and the default `private-key`
# rule matches the PEM header regardless of the body.
